+## Bounded Staleness
+
+{{< public-preview />}}
+
+Bounded Staleness provides the guarantees of [Serializable](#serializable)
+while also placing an explicit upper bound on how stale the data returned by a
+query can be. It is useful when you want fresh data without paying the latency
+of waiting for every input to catch up, and you can tolerate a small, known
+amount of staleness.
+
+You specify the bound as a duration when setting the isolation level, for
+example `bounded staleness 5s`. The duration accepts humantime-style values
+such as `500ms`, `5s`, or `1m30s`. A duration of zero is rejected.
+
+Under Bounded Staleness, Materialize selects the most recent timestamp that
+does not require waiting on input frontiers, subject to the constraint that the
+chosen timestamp is at most the specified duration behind the timestamp oracle.
+If no such timestamp exists within that window, the query fails with a
+`BoundedStalenessExceeded` error (SQLSTATE `40001`) that reports the gap and the
+slowest input.
+
+### Restrictions
+
+-   Bounded Staleness sessions are **read-only**. Statements that write data
+    (`INSERT`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, and `COPY FROM`) are rejected.
+-   Bounded Staleness cannot be combined with
+    [`real_time_recency`](#real-time-recency). Setting `real_time_recency = on`
+    while using a Bounded Staleness isolation level is rejected.
+-   Queries that touch timelines other than the wall-clock
+    (`EpochMilliseconds`) timeline are not supported and return an error.
+-   The feature is gated behind the `enable_bounded_staleness_isolation`
+    feature flag. Until an operator enables it, setting the isolation level
+    errors.
+
